From 51890f7b07cb7f2ea071fccacb96a8470fd063ab Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Andrei Gosman <andrei.gosman@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 2 Oct 2026 14:28:35 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] utils: keep the Linux-only process and thread interfaces
 under __linux__

The utils library did not compile on Darwin for five reasons, none of
them needed by a gNB running over the RF simulator:

  system.c:223:10: fatal error: 'linux/capability.h' file not found
  time_meas.h:15:10: fatal error: 'linux/kernel.h' file not found
  tuntap_if.c:14:10: fatal error: 'linux/netlink.h' file not found
  thread-pool.c:12:10: fatal error: 'sys/sysinfo.h' file not found
  time_meas.c:279:50: error: use of undeclared identifier 'SCHED_IDLE'
  vcd_signal_dumper.c:17:10: fatal error: 'error.h' file not found

has_cap_sys_nice() without libcap read the capability set through a raw
Linux syscall; keep that under __linux__ and answer false elsewhere, so
that threads are created with default priority and no pinning, the same
as an unprivileged Linux process. threadCreate() and thread_top_init()
keep their cpu_set_t handling under __linux__ and report that pinning is
not available. The glibc headers linux/kernel.h, linux/types.h,
sys/sysinfo.h and error.h are included only where they exist; nothing in
these files uses them except get_nprocs(), which darwin_compat.h now
provides over sysconf(). darwin_compat.h also accepts the two-argument
pthread_setname_np() for the calling thread; Darwin cannot name another
thread, and threadCreate() says so instead of asserting. SCHED_IDLE is a
Linux policy: the CPU measurement thread keeps the default policy where
it does not exist.

tuntap_if.c is written on the Linux tun driver, ioctl(2) interface
configuration and ip(8) rules. Only the UE reaches it. On other
platforms every function reports failure; utun on Darwin is a separate
step.
